  <p>
    PL-8177 is a good reminder that the melanocortin system does more than pigment and
    appetite. Its third great role is <strong>calming inflammation</strong> — the natural
    signal <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> is a potent anti-inflammatory molecule — and
    PL-8177 is an attempt to turn that biology into a drug for inflammatory bowel
    disease.<Cite n={8} /> It is one of Palatin Technologies' investigational melanocortin
    agents: a synthetic cyclic heptapeptide that is a potent and selective{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> agonist, activating the human receptor at
    low-nanomolar concentrations while leaving MC3R, MC4R and MC5R largely
    alone.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Stays where it lands. A polymer-coated capsule carries PL-8177 past the stomach and small intestine and releases it in the colon, where it acts on MC1R in the inflamed lining before leaving in the faeces. In the human microdose study nothing reached plasma or urine."
    source="Schematic, after Dodd et al. 2023."
  >
    <StaysWhereItLands />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Why MC1R, and what "resolution" means</h2>
  <p>
    The receptor choice is deliberate. MC1R is the melanocortin receptor through which
    α-MSH does much of its anti-inflammatory work on immune cells, and a selective agonist
    can engage it without touching the appetite receptor{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> or the adrenal <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>.
    In the laboratory PL-8177 activates mouse and human MC1R at picomolar-to-nanomolar
    concentrations, and on macrophages it does two distinct things: it reduces the
    release of the inflammatory cytokines IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α and CCL2, and it increases
    <strong>efferocytosis</strong>, the clearing of dead cells that lets inflamed tissue
    return to normal rather than merely go quiet.<Cite n={2} /> That second action is
    what distinguishes a <em>pro-resolving</em> drug from an anti-inflammatory one, and
    it is the central idea of the <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page. In a
    mouse model of inflammatory arthritis, PL-8177 reduced clinical score, paw swelling
    and the recruitment of immune cells into the joint<Cite n={2} /> — evidence that the
    mechanism works in vivo, well before the gut was chosen as the first indication.
  </p>

  <h2>An oral peptide that doesn't need to be absorbed</h2>
  <p>
    The clever part is the delivery. Peptides are usually poor oral drugs because the gut
    digests them before they can reach the bloodstream — but PL-8177 turns that problem
    into a non-issue by <em>not trying to reach the bloodstream at all</em>. It is
    enclosed in a polymer formulation that carries it through the stomach and small
    intestine and releases it in the colon. In rats and dogs, orally dosed PL-8177 was
    found at higher concentrations in the colon than in the upper gut; in a phase 0
    study in healthy men given a 70-microgram dose of radiolabelled drug, PL-8177 and its
    main metabolite were detected in the faeces and not at all in plasma or urine, which
    means the peptide was released from its coat and acted and was broken down entirely
    within the gut.<Cite n={1} /> Because its target — MC1R on the colon's immune and
    epithelial cells — is right there, staying in the gut is a feature, not a failure. It
    is the exception that clarifies the rule explained on the{' '}
    <a href="/oral-peptides">oral peptides</a> page: oral peptides struggle when they
    need to travel, not when they act where they land.
  </p>

  <h2>What it did in the animal colon</h2>
  <p>
    In two rat models of colitis, oral PL-8177 at a 50-microgram dose lowered macroscopic
    colon damage scores and improved colon weight, stool consistency and occult bleeding
    compared with vehicle. Under the microscope the treated colons kept their structure
    and barrier, with less immune-cell infiltration and more intact enterocytes. The
    most telling result came from single-nucleus RNA sequencing of the tissue: treatment
    moved the colon's cell populations and gene-expression profile back toward those of
    healthy controls, with immune-marker genes and immune pathways
    under-represented relative to untreated disease.<Cite n={1} /> That is the molecular
    signature of resolution rather than suppression, and it set up the human trial.
  </p>

  <h2>The ulcerative colitis signal</h2>
  <p>
    PL-8177's lead programme is in <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong>, an inflammatory
    disease of the colon. A randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase 2 study
    gave once-daily oral PL-8177 for eight weeks to patients with active disease. The
    topline, reported in 2025, was clinical remission in 33% of treated patients (three
    of nine) against none on placebo, clinical response in 78% against 33%, and
    symptomatic remission in 56% against 33%; among patients with moderate disease, most
    showed improvement across all three segments of the colon examined. No adverse
    events were attributed to the drug.<Cite n={3} /> The honest caveat matters, though:
    Palatin stopped enrolment at 12 patients — nine on drug, three on placebo — rather
    than the 28 planned, as it redirected resources toward its melanocortin obesity
    programmes and out-licensing discussions.<Cite n={4} /> With those numbers the study
    was not powered to demonstrate statistical significance, so the result is an
    encouraging signal rather than proof. The developer's own framing is that the
    programme is now available for partnering, which is where it stands in
    2026.<Cite n={7} />
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the gut</h2>
  <p>
    The same gut-restricted idea does not carry to other tissues, but the molecule does.
    PL-8177 holds an FDA orphan-drug designation for non-infectious uveitis,<Cite n={6} />
    and in a mouse model of autoimmune uveitis it suppressed the disease and induced
    the regulatory immune response that follows — though, unlike α-MSH or a pan-agonist,
    its effect was temporary, which the authors read as evidence that ocular
    inflammation needs MC5R as well as MC1R.<Cite n={5} /> It is the finding that
    explains why Palatin's eye drop, <a href="/pl-9643">PL-9643</a>, is a pan-agonist
    while its gut drug is MC1R-selective: the receptor profile is chosen per tissue.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    PL-8177 is early, and its own developer has shifted focus, so its future depends on a
    partner. But it is a clean illustration of the melanocortin system's pro-resolving
    arm being engineered into a drug — with the mechanism shown on cells, in arthritic
    joints and in inflamed colons before a small, promising human trial — and of a smart
    way around the oral-peptide problem when the target sits in the gut. For the biology
    it draws on, see <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">the anti-inflammatory arm</a>; for where it sits among
    everything in development, see the <a href="/pipeline">melanocortin pipeline</a>.
  </p>

